From b371d8549545b9a13fa262faa480b0c43cb0db3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ben Vanik <ben.vanik@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 18:44:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [Loom] Preserve ABI preambles during allocation rewrites

Fixed-input splitting and profitable source replication must place their
transfers after every entry live-in and resource declaration, not
immediately after an individual definition. Retain the preamble boundary
in the existing function inventory and carry that borrowed anchor into
allocation so both rewrite paths preserve the same invariant without
another IR traversal.

Keep preamble operands on the original input while rewriting later body
operands to the split result. Ordinary definitions and nonentry block
arguments retain their existing insertion points, and the change leaves
register budgets, profitability decisions, and repair convergence policy
intact.

Backport of upstream 5443bf71813735e0c6add08f162672dfaa96ecfc (#1377)
onto 5927b0e0. This revision has no function requirements inventory, so
the function model records the entry preamble end from the entry block's
leading live-in/resource prefix, which Low verification keeps contiguous.
The split path keeps this revision's single-split result. loom-check
verifies the module again after a successful provider emission.
entry_preamble.loom-test drops its two with-checks allocation-json cases,
which this loom-check cannot run, and its split expectations are this
revision's allocator output. low_allocation.loom-test keeps the
live_in_split_follows_entry_preamble case, which splits a fixed live-in
under SGPR pressure at this revision.
